## The dirham, a currency you get on the spot

Morocco's currency is the **dirham** (MAD), divided into 100 centimes. It comes in 20, 50, 100 and 200 dirham notes, and in coins.

The dirham is not freely convertible: it is in principle difficult, or even impossible, to obtain before you leave. Regulations also limit the amount of dirhams you can bring into or take out of the country. So only exchange what you need, and check the current rules with the Office des Changes before your trip.

{% tip title="Rials and dirhams" %}
In conversation, some shopkeepers still count in "rials" (20 rials to 1 dirham). If you are unsure about a price, ask which currency is meant.
{% /tip %}

## Exchanging money

- **Exchange offices** and **banks** are plentiful in cities and at airports. Euros are easy to exchange.
- Compare the rates displayed and check that there is no commission.
- **Keep your exchange receipts**: they are generally required to change your remaining dirhams back before you leave the country.

## Cash machines and bank cards

Cash machines are common in cities and tourist areas. They are rarer in villages, mountain valleys and on the edge of the desert: withdraw cash before heading to these areas.

Bank cards are accepted in most hotels, in supermarkets and in many restaurants in the big cities. They are rarely accepted in souks, small shops, taxis and small eateries.

{% warning title="Before you leave" %}
Tell your bank about your trip to avoid your card being blocked, and check the fees for withdrawals and payments abroad.
{% /warning %}

## Cash: good habits

- Always keep **small notes** and coins: shopkeepers do not always have change for a large note.
- Spread your money between several pockets or bags, and do not take out a large wad of notes in public.
- Count your change calmly and without embarrassment.

## Tipping

Tipping is not compulsory, but it is customary as a thank you for a service: a guide, a driver, riad staff, a porter or a waiter. A small amount, given discreetly, is always appreciated.

For your shopping, read our tips for [haggling in the souks](/en/guides/haggling-in-the-souks).
